# Vue coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to Vue Single File
Component conventions. Applies only to `*.vue` files -- the `<script>`
block's own TypeScript style still comes from `ts-js-node`'s coding-style
rule, which this file `extends` transitively through the pack's `extends`.

## Script setup and typing

- Use `<script setup lang="ts">` for new components; do not introduce the
  Options API or a bare (non-setup) `<script>` block in new files unless the
  project has no `<script setup>` components at all yet.
- Declare props with the type-only form, `defineProps<{ ... }>()`, not the
  runtime-object form (`defineProps({ foo: String })`), so prop types are
  checked against actual usage instead of a separate runtime shape. Use
  `withDefaults(defineProps<Props>(), { ... })` for default values rather
  than mutating the returned props object.
- Declare emits with the type-only form: `defineEmits<{ change: [id: number]
  }>()` (named-tuple syntax) rather than the untyped string-array form
  (`defineEmits(['change'])`).
- On Vue 3.5+ projects, destructuring `defineProps()` directly
  (`const { title } = defineProps<Props>()`) is reactive and preferred over
  a separate `props.title` reference; on pre-3.5 projects, keep the props
  object intact (`props.title`, or `toRefs(props)`) since a destructured
  prop is a frozen snapshot, not a live binding.
- Type `ref<T>()` explicitly when the initial value's inferred type is
  narrower than the eventual type (`ref<string | null>(null)`, not a bare
  `ref(null)` that infers `Ref<null>`).

## Naming and structure

- Component file and `name` (when set) in `PascalCase`
  (`OrderSummary.vue`); composable files and exported function names in
  `camelCase` prefixed with `use` (`useOrderTotals.ts`).
- One component per `.vue` file; a template that needs its own local
  sub-markup repeated more than once becomes its own child component, not a
  `v-for` over inline duplicated markup.
- Order SFC blocks `<script setup>`, `<template>`, `<style>` (or the
  project's existing established order) consistently across the codebase;
  do not invent a third ordering in new files.
- Multi-word component names (`UserCard`, not `Card`) to avoid clashing
  with current and future HTML elements, matching `eslint-plugin-vue`'s
  `multi-word-component-names` rule.

## Formatting and imports

- Format with the project's configured formatter (Prettier or the
  project's own config); do not hand-format around a formatter that is
  already configured.
- Run the project's configured `eslint` (with `eslint-plugin-vue`) instead
  of hand-checking template directive order, unused template refs, or
  v-for/v-if precedence; fix what it flags rather than adding a blanket
  disable comment.
- Prefer auto-imported composables/components only when the project has
  `unplugin-auto-import`/`unplugin-vue-components` (or Nuxt) configured;
  otherwise import explicitly -- do not assume auto-import is active.

## Errors

- Surface a composable or store action's failure to the caller (thrown
  error, or a returned error/result shape the caller checks) instead of
  swallowing it in a bare `catch` with no rethrow and no user-visible
  state change.
- Use Vue's `onErrorCaptured` (component-tree scope) or a top-level
  `app.config.errorHandler` (app-wide scope) for error boundaries, not an
  ad hoc `try/catch` wrapped around every template-triggered call site.
